**Generic array in C++.** Templates make it direct: the compiler generates code for each type.
```cpp
template <typename T>
class MyArray {
  T* data; size_t n;
public:
  explicit MyArray(size_t n) : data(new T[n]()), n(n) {}
  ~MyArray() { delete[] data; }
  T& operator[](size_t i) { return data[i]; }
};
```
(In practice use `std::vector<T>`; add copy/move operations or delete them, following the rule of five.)

**Generic array in Java.** `new T[n]` is **illegal**, because generics use **type erasure**: `T` does not exist at runtime, and arrays must know their element type. Workarounds:
```java
class MyArray<T> {
  private final T[] data;
  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
  MyArray(int n) { data = (T[]) new Object[n]; }        // unchecked cast
  MyArray(Class<T> type, int n) {                       // type-safe
    data = (T[]) java.lang.reflect.Array.newInstance(type, n);
  }
}
```
Or simply use `ArrayList<T>`. Arrays are covariant and reified while generics are invariant and erased, so mixing them is unsafe.

**Generics versus templates**
- Java: one compiled class shared by all types; checks happen at compile time, erased at runtime; type arguments must be reference types (no `List<int>`, use `Integer`); bounded types via `extends`.
- C++: a separate instantiation per type at compile time; works with primitives; can specialise; supports non-type parameters and compile-time computation; error messages are harder, and code size can grow.

**Constructors**
- Java: a default constructor is provided if you declare none; constructor chaining with `this(...)` and `super(...)`; no destructors (the garbage collector reclaims memory); no copy constructor by default.
- C++: has copy and move constructors, initialiser lists and destructors (RAII); you manage object lifetime; constructors cannot be virtual.

**Other differences**
- Memory: garbage collection versus manual or RAII management; Java has no raw pointers.
- Compilation: bytecode on a JVM versus native code.
- Inheritance: Java has single class inheritance plus interfaces; C++ has multiple inheritance; Java methods are virtual by default, C++ ones only when marked `virtual`.
